{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfhbx9CPAVvjJ26Zab5ux","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate `nonredundant_periodicity_representativeness`, and it passed the admission self-check (`OK`). It refines the incumbent `iter0_candidate3`.\n\n**What changed:** the original penalised streaks and perfect alternation twice. Once through the balance and alternation-rate cues, and again as a \"periodic template\", because all-same flips (period 1) and HTHT… (period 2) also match the template scan perfectly. Now the periodic-pattern penalty only counts repeating templates of period 3 or more, such as HHT-HHT or HHTT-HHTT. Streaks and plain alternation are judged only by the balance and alternation cues. Everything else is kept as it was, including the person-specific sensitivity.\n\n**Why:** the critique's one FDR-surviving discrepancy (q = 0.016) is that the incumbent over-penalises perfect alternation. People chose it 0.768 of the time against the model's predicted 0.858. Less strongly (q = 0.069), the incumbent also over-penalises long runs. Removing the double count should ease both.\n\n**Self-check result:** the smoke fit gave an ELPD-LOO of −1019.4, against the incumbent's −1023.0. That fit used only 100 draws on one chain, so this is a hint, not a result. The check also left some things open:\n- It couldn't measure convergence (R-hat needs more than one chain).\n- PSIS-LOO was unreliable on that short fit: 7.7% of trials had a high Pareto k, against a 1% tolerance. Admission's full fit will show whether that persists.\n- It doesn't test novelty. Admission will check whether the predictions differ enough from the incumbent's across the stimulus space.\n\nThe three files are in `experiment1/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_4/`: `hypothesis.md`, `model_name.txt` and `candidate.py`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cac